 ## Overview
-This method computes the gradient of the rendered image with respect to scene parameters $\theta$ by integrating differential measurement contributions over path space [S3]. It addresses the limitation of unidirectional estimators, which produce noisy gradients and inaccurate reconstructions in scenes with hard-to-sample lights and specular materials [S1]. The method distinguishes itself by combining Multiple Importance Sampling (MIS) with Next Event Estimation (NEE) to allow the differential vertex to be located at any intermediate vertex or at the emitter connection, rather than being restricted to the final vertex as in standard NEE [S3].
+This method computes the gradient of the rendered image with respect to scene parameters $\theta$ by integrating differential measurement contributions over path space [S1]. It solves the problem of high variance in gradient estimation for specular materials and complex lighting by combining standard BRDF sampling with Next Event Estimation (NEE) using Multiple Importance Sampling (MIS) [S1]. Unlike standard NEE which only connects paths to emitters at the final vertex, this method allows the differential vertex (the location where the parameter derivative is applied) to be located at any intermediate vertex $i$ or at the emitter connection, combining BRDF and emitter sampling probabilities into a single mixture weight [S1]. It builds on [Reparameterization Estimators](6499-reparameterization-estimators.md) by extending the reparameterization principle to full path-space integrals with explicit MIS weights for differential contributions.
 
 ## How it works
-The estimator computes $\partial_\theta I$ by generating standard paths and NEE paths, calculating differential contributions for all possible differential vertices, and weighting them by a mixture probability that accounts for both BRDF sampling and emitter sampling strategies [S3].
-
